          "first": {
            "seq": 9,
            "text": "Translate a Chinese technical README into English and write the result to a new file.\n\nSOURCE FILE (read it fully — it is ~53 KB, ~476 lines, UTF-8):\nC:\\Users\\hoang\\OneDrive\\Documents\\deepseek-harness\\default-workspace\\DeepSeek-Balance-Whale-Widget\\README.md\n\nOUTPUT FILE (create this, do not modify the source):\nC:\\Users\\hoang\\OneDrive\\Documents\\deepseek-harness\\default-workspace\\DeepSeek-Balance-Whale-Widget\\README.en.md\n\nThis is the README for `dsh-whale-widget`, a plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH) that shows a whale-girl widget in the bottom-right corner of the DSH web UI displaying the DeepSeek API balance, today's spend, and per-turn cost.\n\nTRANSLATION REQUIREMENTS — follow these exactly:\n\n1. Translate ALL prose into natural, fluent English. This is developer documentation, so use a clear technical register, not literal word-for-word translation. Where a Chinese idiom or colloquial phrase (e.g. 喵~, 弹弹, 一键) does not carry over, convey the meaning naturally in English rather than transliterating.\n\n2. DO NOT translate or alter, under any circumstances:\n   - Code blocks, shell commands, CLI flags, and their comments where the comment is a command example\n   - File paths, directory names, environment variables, URLs\n   - Package names (`dsh-whale-widget`, `@deepseek-ai/dsh-base`, etc.)\n   - JSON / YAML / configuration keys and values\n   - Error messages quoted verbatim from the software (e.g. `error: profile \"desktop\" is managed exclusively by the Electron application`) — keep these in English exactly as they appear\n   - Version numbers, commit SHAs, issue/PR numbers\n   - The `![...](assets/...)` image references — keep the image paths byte-identical; you may translate only the alt text\n   \n3. PRESERVE THE STRUCTURE EXACTLY:\n   - All heading levels (#, ##, ###) in the same order, with the same numbering\n   - All Markdown tables — keep the same number of rows and columns, same order\n   - All blockquotes (>), lists, bold (**), and inline code (`) markers\n   - All emoji (⚠️, ✅, ❌, etc.) in their original positions\n   - The approximate total line count — do not summarize, condense, or omit any section. The output must be a COMPLETE translation of every section, including the long \"给 AI 的安装说明\" (installation instructions for AI assistants) section and the FAQ, pricing table, and acknowledgements.\n\n4. Keep the title line's structure: `# DSH Whale Balance Widget (DeepSeek Balance Whale Widget)` — translate the Chinese name but keep the English parenthetical that is already there.\n\n5. The document contains some code comments in Chinese (inside bash blocks). Translate those comments to English, since they are explanatory prose — but never alter the command itself on the same line.\n\nWORKFLOW:\n- Read the entire source file first (use offset/limit to page through it if needed — it is about 476 lines).\n- Write the complete English translation to the output path in one `write` call.\n- Then re-read your output file and verify: (a) every heading from the source is present, (b) the tables have the same row counts, (c) no code block was altered.\n- Report back: the output path, total line count, and any section you were unsure about.\nYour parent agent id is \"session-5f849a2d-cf55-4d27-9f63-051eb23432de\". Before you finish, send your result to that agent with send_message({ agent_id: \"session-5f849a2d-cf55-4d27-9f63-051eb23432de\", message: \"<self-contained result>\" }). The parent shares your workspace but does not automatically receive your transcript, tool output, or reasoning. Send earlier messages as well when a finding changes what the parent should do next; sending a message does not end your turn."
          },
